{
  "dom_id": "step-q344973",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume increase when height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
